<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Hindi (hi). Paired with values/strings.xml key for key.

    Two rules hold this file together, and both are checked by
    tools/verify-android-strings.py on every build:

      Every key here exists in the default file. A key that does not is dead weight
      that reads as a translated string somebody can never see.

      Every format specifier matches the English original exactly, including its
      position. A Hindi string that loses a %1$s does not look wrong - it throws
      IllegalFormatException the moment that screen opens, in Hindi only, on a
      phone in a village.

    Banking acronyms stay in English (NPA, OTS, KRM, CKCC, KYC, PAN, CIF, GPS, RBI,
    APY, PMJJBY, PMSBY, PMJDY). That is what the paper forms say and what branch
    staff say out loud; translating them would make the app harder to use, not
    easier, and would stop matching the printed report.
-->
